## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of a classic bit-twiddling pattern to find the next permutation of a fixed-popcount bitset in constant time, without loops or branches. It also probes edge cases like overflow and zero input.

## Key Concepts

- Snoob algorithm for next higher with same popcount
- 64-bit overflow behavior and edge cases (n == 0 or max with k ones)
- Pure bitwise/shifts approach, no loops

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t nextWithSamePopcount(uint64_t n) {
  uint64_t smallest = n & -n;
  uint64_t ripple = n + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = n ^ ripple;
  return ripple | ((ones >> 2) / smallest);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to also return the index of the isolated bit in a 0..127 range?
- What are the edge-case behaviors when x is zero or has a single 1 at the MSB?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests depth in bit twiddling and constant-time operations; parity computation is fundamental in checksums, error detection, cryptography, and performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts
- Parity reduction across word boundaries by folding upper and lower halves
- Fixed-time parallel reduction using shifts and XOR
- No branches; constant-time execution regardless of input

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t p = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  p ^= p >> 32;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to arbitrary bit widths or for streaming parity computation?

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced bit manipulation questions assess knowledge of branchless arithmetic and de Bruijn indexing techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Bit manipulation: isolating the least significant bit using two's complement arithmetic
- De Bruijn sequence mapping for constant-time position lookup
- Branchless, constant-time operations
- Proper edge-case handling for n == 0

## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses branchless bit-twiddling across multiword data and solid understanding of SWAR popcount. 

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (single-instruction, multiple-data) population count
- 64-bit limb handling within a 128-bit composite
- Branchless design using masks and shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t loCount = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t hiCount = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  return (int)(loCount + hiCount);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize for wide vectors (e.g., 256-bit) with minimal branching?
- How would you validate correctness across edge cases like zero and all-ones?
